1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,955
Japan has one point nine five million
drink machines, and they are vanishing.

2
00:00:05,455 --> 00:00:08,711
In twenty fourteen, there were two point
four seven million.

3
00:00:09,211 --> 00:00:11,053
So where did all those machines go?

4
00:00:11,073 --> 00:00:14,371
Stay until the end, because the answer
involves an earthquake.

5
00:00:14,871 --> 00:00:17,345
This is Japan, the Land of the Rising Sun.

6
00:00:17,845 --> 00:00:20,604
About one hundred twenty-three million
people liv here,

7
00:00:20,624 --> 00:00:22,797
and a machine waits on almost every
corner.

8
00:00:23,297 --> 00:00:25,861
Why so many? The usual answer is low
crime.

9
00:00:25,881 --> 00:00:28,835
Nobody smashes a machine left alone on a
street.

10
00:00:29,335 --> 00:00:31,865
Land and workers are expensive too, so a
machine

11
00:00:31,885 --> 00:00:34,223
that needs no shop and no clerk makes
sense.

12
00:00:34,723 --> 00:00:37,400
The idea is old. The first Japanese patent
for a vending

13
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:40,166
machine was filed in eighteen
eighty-eight, for tobacco.

14
00:00:40,666 --> 00:00:43,080
That same trust shows up in lost property.

15
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,979
In twenty twenty-five, Tokyo police
received about

16
00:00:45,999 --> 00:00:48,375
four point five billion yen in lost cash.

17
00:00:48,875 --> 00:00:51,255
That is more than half of the money people
reported

18
00:00:51,275 --> 00:00:53,581
lost, and a new high for the fourth year
running.

19
00:00:54,081 --> 00:00:57,020
More than one hundred forty-four thousand
phones were handed in,

20
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:00,092
and about one hundred nineteen thousand
went back to their owners.

21
00:01:00,592 --> 00:01:03,457
So if machines are this safe, why are they
vanishing?

22
00:01:03,957 --> 00:01:06,021
Convenience stores are the first suspect.

23
00:01:06,041 --> 00:01:08,156
There are about fifty-six thousand now, up

24
00:01:08,176 --> 00:01:10,666
from about fifty-one thousand in twenty
fourteen.

25
00:01:11,166 --> 00:01:13,114
Profits are falling while prices rise.

26
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:16,864
Even the industry research group says
profitability keeps getting worse.

27
00:01:17,364 --> 00:01:19,628
DyDo plans to remove about twenty thousand
of its

28
00:01:19,648 --> 00:01:23,144
roughly two hundred seventy thousand
machines by early twenty twenty-seven.

29
00:01:23,644 --> 00:01:26,376
Pokka Sapporo once ran about one hundred
thousand machines.

30
00:01:26,396 --> 00:01:29,427
Now it has about forty thousand, and it is
selling that business.

31
00:01:29,927 --> 00:01:31,950
But the machines are not just dying.

32
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:32,992
They are changing.

33
00:01:33,492 --> 00:01:36,757
Some now sell frozen gyoza, ramen, and
even caviar.

34
00:01:37,257 --> 00:01:39,466
And then there is what they do when the
ground shakes.

35
00:01:39,966 --> 00:01:42,715
After the twenty eleven earthquake, more
than four hundred Coca

36
00:01:42,735 --> 00:01:45,680
Cola machines gave away over eighty-eight
thousand drinks for free.

37
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:48,148
In the city of Ako, some machines unlock

38
00:01:48,168 --> 00:01:50,355
for free when an evacuation order is
issued.

39
00:01:50,855 --> 00:01:54,767
Others carry backup batteries, so they
keep working when the power fails.

40
00:01:55,267 --> 00:01:58,823
So the machines are vanishing, but the
idea is not.

41
00:01:58,843 --> 00:02:02,348
A machine you can trust, and that trusts
you back.

42
00:02:02,848 --> 00:02:05,360
That was Japan. Which country should we
visit next?

43
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:06,595
Tell me in the comments.

